WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:20.790
The F-35B is arguably the most advanced plane
ever made.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:22.760
A jack of all trades.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:29.930
A stealth fighter plane, combining and improving
on the capabilities of the F-16, AV-8B Harrier,

00:00:29.930 --> 00:00:31.270
and B-2.

00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:36.480
A highly maneuverable fighter plane capable
of attacking both airborne and ground based

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.480
targets.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:41.800
A stealth fighter, taking the lessons learned
from Lockheed’s previous ventures into stealth,

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.430
with the F-22 raptor and F-117 Nighthawk.

00:00:45.430 --> 00:00:50.000
A plane fitted with the most advanced sensors
and computer systems.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:55.079
Sharing information almost instantly with
allies, without compromising stealth, and

00:00:55.079 --> 00:00:59.870
presenting the information directly on a heads
up display on the pilots helmet visor.

00:00:59.870 --> 00:01:02.960
Giving unparalleled situational awareness.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:07.640
Allowing a flight of F-35s to effectively
fight as a hivemind.

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:13.460
Perhaps most boldly of all, the F-35B is capable
of transitioning from horizontal to vertical

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:19.670
flight with a push of a button, using directional
thrust and a massive vertical turbofan engine,

00:01:19.670 --> 00:01:21.440
hidden within the plane's body.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.030
Making it possible to land like a helicopter
on the relatively small amphibious assault

00:01:26.030 --> 00:01:38.370
ships of the US Marines.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:43.750
Fitting all these capabilities into a single
airframe is an extremely difficult task.

00:01:43.750 --> 00:01:48.740
Designing for stealth demands careful molding
of the exterior of the plane, dictating the

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:51.170
design of crucial features.

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:52.680
Creating unavoidable trade-offs.

00:01:52.680 --> 00:01:56.170
There are a lot of misconceptions about stealth.

00:01:56.170 --> 00:02:01.780
The goal isn’t to make an aircraft invisible,
it will be detectable, the goal is to delay

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:05.210
enemy detection for as long as possible.

00:02:05.210 --> 00:02:10.170
For bomber aircraft, it shrinks the range
of the enemy's radar stations, potentially

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:15.420
opening gaps in radar defenses and allowing
the aircraft to slip through undetected.

00:02:15.420 --> 00:02:20.550
For fighter aircraft it provides a critical
advantage, detect your enemy before they detect

00:02:20.550 --> 00:02:21.590
you.

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:26.410
To gain these advantages, we need to make
it harder for the radar receiver to decipher

00:02:26.410 --> 00:02:30.550
whether the return signal is just background
noise or an enemy aircraft.

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:34.610
To do that we need to minimize the strength
of the return signal.

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:39.270
There are several mechanisms for a radar wave
to be reflected.

00:02:39.270 --> 00:02:43.870
The most significant, and most obvious way
is through specular return, otherwise known

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:46.069
as regular reflection, like a mirror.

00:02:46.069 --> 00:02:49.610
[1] Where the angle of reflection equals the
angle of incidence.

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:55.709
We want to avoid large flat surfaces that
could reflect straight back to the radar receiver.

00:02:55.709 --> 00:03:01.170
Corner reflectors, where two surfaces set
at a 90 degree angle to each other, need to

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:03.340
be avoided at all costs.

00:03:03.340 --> 00:03:08.220
Tailplanes, consisting of the vertical and
horizontal stabilizer, are the perfect surfaces

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:10.900
to create a corner reflector.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:15.450
Allowing radar to bounce off both surfaces
and return right back in the direction it

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:16.450
came.

00:03:16.450 --> 00:03:21.019
The best way to avoid this is to remove the
tail completely, like the B-2, but this impacts

00:03:21.019 --> 00:03:23.410
maneuverability greatly.

00:03:23.410 --> 00:03:27.849
Instead we can replace both the horizontal
and vertical stabilizer with a v-tail, as

00:03:27.849 --> 00:03:30.670
seen on the F-117 Nighthawk.

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:35.450
The v-tail can act as both a rudder and an
elevator, and we can see how by examining

00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:41.260
the resultant force generated when the control
surfaces are actuated to different positions.

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:46.890
We can actuate them in opposite directions
to generate a horizontal resultant force,

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:49.450
providing yaw control as a vertical rudder
would.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:54.019
Or we can deflect them in the same direction
to provide pitch control, as an horizontal

00:03:54.019 --> 00:03:55.490
elevator would.

00:03:55.490 --> 00:04:00.910
This configuration is sometimes used for unique
looking aircraft like the Cirrus SF50, allowing

00:04:00.910 --> 00:04:06.120
it to mount a single tiny jet engine on top
of the fuselage with its exhaust directed

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:07.170
straight through the v-tail.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:13.129
A private jet so tiny and lightweight that
it can deploy a parachute to rescue itself

00:04:13.129 --> 00:04:14.800
in emergencies.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:20.579
Having rudder and elevator controls linked
in a single mechanism like this is not ideal.

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:26.541
Fighter jets like the F-35 and the F-22 need
superior control authority, and that is a

00:04:26.541 --> 00:04:29.160
function of control surface area.

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:32.030
The larger the elevator the larger the pitch
control.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:37.419
If a control surface is working a double role,
where rudder and elevator action is needed

00:04:37.419 --> 00:04:40.650
at the same time, it reduces the control authority.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:46.690
So, both planes also feature large elevators,
offset by a distance and angled to prevent

00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:48.370
corner reflections.

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:54.890
We can see many more trade offs in design
by comparing the F-117 and F-35 in the quest

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:58.440
to fulfill both stealth and fighter requirements.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:03.970
Both the F-117 and B-2 have their engine air
intakes mounted on the upper surface of the

00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:08.870
plane, which prevents ground based radar from
bouncing around inside the intake and back

00:05:08.870 --> 00:05:12.380
to the receiver, and helps reduce infrared
heat signatures.

00:05:12.380 --> 00:05:17.950
However, for a plane expected to make high
angle of attack maneuvers in life and death

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:21.180
situations, this isn’t a design you would
want to include

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:26.419
While performing a maneuver like this the
air intake will receive lower pressure air,

00:05:26.419 --> 00:05:30.320
which will lower performance right when performance
is needed most.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:35.810
Air intakes located underneath the aircraft,
like the F-16, will cause too much radar return,

00:05:35.810 --> 00:05:40.860
so twin intakes located on either side of
the fuselage are instead chosen.

00:05:40.860 --> 00:05:45.310
The air intake has some clever aerodynamic
features too.

00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:49.000
This seemingly innocuous bump plays an important
role.

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:54.160
This is aeronautical engineering epitomized,
every seemingly insignificant design feature

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:55.790
has a purpose.

00:05:55.790 --> 00:06:00.400
Mounting engine intakes along the body of
an aircraft comes with some issues that pylon

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.410
mounted engines avoid.

00:06:02.410 --> 00:06:07.229
As air travels along the length of the body,
air begins to form a layer of slow moving

00:06:07.229 --> 00:06:10.030
turbulent air called a boundary layer.

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:15.139
If this air is allowed to enter the engine
it not only lowers performance, it can also

00:06:15.139 --> 00:06:16.780
damage the engine.

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:21.220
As the turbine rotates it will pass through
the slow boundary air on one side and then

00:06:21.220 --> 00:06:23.860
fast free stream air on the other.

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:29.340
This means the force on the turbine blade
changes for each and every rotation, causing

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:30.389
cyclical bending.

00:06:30.389 --> 00:06:33.190
A recipe for fatigue failure.

00:06:33.190 --> 00:06:38.009
Planes like the F-16 feature a boundary layer
diverter that separates the inlet from the

00:06:38.009 --> 00:06:44.500
fuselage with a small gap, but this design
increases radar cross-section and increases

00:06:44.500 --> 00:06:45.500
drag.

00:06:45.500 --> 00:06:49.190
[2]
Later variants of the F-16 tested the DSI,

00:06:49.190 --> 00:06:51.990
a diverterless supersonic inlet.

00:06:51.990 --> 00:06:57.180
Essentially a large bump that creates a region
of compression that pushes the boundary layer

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:01.940
away from the inlet, while also scattering
incoming radar and lowering drag.

00:07:01.940 --> 00:07:07.110
The test flight demonstrated it could meet
performance requirements, ushering its introduction

00:07:07.110 --> 00:07:08.970
to the F-35.

00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:14.280
The final product reduces weight by 30% and
lowers production and maintenance costs.

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:18.639
Moving down the plane we can see other hints
of stealth design.

00:07:18.639 --> 00:07:21.430
Long sharp edges are the enemy of stealth.

00:07:21.430 --> 00:07:26.889
Sharp edges cause radar waves to scatter in
all directions, radar can even travel along

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:32.180
the length of a surface in the form of a traveling
wave, and then scatter upon reaching the trailing

00:07:32.180 --> 00:07:33.180
edge.

00:07:33.180 --> 00:07:37.790
The strength of that return signal can be
reduced with a few clever techniques.

00:07:37.790 --> 00:07:42.509
The strength of the signal will depend on
the length of the edge, so the first technique

00:07:42.509 --> 00:07:44.960
is to reduce the edge length with serration.

00:07:44.960 --> 00:07:50.360
You can see this technique very clearly on
the trailing edges of the B-2.

00:07:50.360 --> 00:07:55.509
However, it’s less obvious where it’s
used for the F-35, until you start looking

00:07:55.509 --> 00:08:01.020
at all the access hatches hidden around the
aircraft, every single unavoidable surface

00:08:01.020 --> 00:08:04.300
gap on the aircraft has a serrated edge.

00:08:04.300 --> 00:08:09.889
This hatch opens to reveal a telescoping ladder
to allow pilots to climb in and out of the

00:08:09.889 --> 00:08:10.889
aircraft.

00:08:10.889 --> 00:08:12.910
These open to reveal the landing gear.

00:08:12.910 --> 00:08:16.990
These are the internal weapons bays, essential
for keeping the radar reflecting missiles

00:08:16.990 --> 00:08:22.210
hidden from view, and t hese smaller hatches
are flare dispenser doors.

00:08:22.210 --> 00:08:26.879
Flares are effective decoys for heat seeking
missiles, but they do nothing to prevent radar

00:08:26.879 --> 00:08:28.470
guided missiles.

00:08:28.470 --> 00:08:34.200
In the face of radar guided missile proliferation
and continued growing sophistication in the

00:08:34.200 --> 00:08:37.839
technology, a new decoy system was developed.

00:08:37.839 --> 00:08:41.040
This new technology is released from this
panel.

00:08:41.040 --> 00:08:46.950
When needed this access door opens and a transmitter
begins to reel out to a safe distance behind

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:47.950
the plane.

00:08:47.950 --> 00:08:52.840
It has 3 levels of countermeasures to protect
the F-35 from attack.

00:08:52.840 --> 00:08:57.200
First it actively jams missiles while they
attempt to lock onto their target,

00:08:57.200 --> 00:09:02.500
by emitting jamming signals which the onboard
computer computes and delivers to the emitter

00:09:02.500 --> 00:09:04.720
through the fiber optic tow line.

00:09:04.720 --> 00:09:09.210
If the radar manages to obtain a lock, it
then begins to attempt to break the lock.

00:09:09.210 --> 00:09:13.360
Disrupting the tracking algorithms guiding
the missile towards its target.

00:09:13.360 --> 00:09:18.740
Finally, if all is lost and the missile is
bearing in on the aircraft, the emitter begins

00:09:18.740 --> 00:09:24.709
to simulate the aircraft's radar signature,
drawing the missile towards it as a decoy.

00:09:24.709 --> 00:09:27.560
[3]
These serrated hatches hid critical components

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:33.459
of the F-35, but more can be done to reduce
edge scattering in these locations.

00:09:33.459 --> 00:09:38.459
You may notice that the color around these
edges is different to the rest of the plane.

00:09:38.459 --> 00:09:43.400
This is because the edges are treated with
a special radar scattering tape.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:47.520
In the same way traveling waves scatter when
they meet an edge discontinuity, they will

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:50.860
scatter when traveling over a change in conductivity.

00:09:50.860 --> 00:09:57.001
The F-35 uses this to its advantage to scatter
the waves over a longer distance, reducing

00:09:57.001 --> 00:09:59.690
the return signal by spreading it out.

00:09:59.690 --> 00:10:05.120
The tape has a conductivity gradient, gradually
decreasing its electrical conductivity, causing

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:07.899
radar waves to scatter at each interval.

00:10:07.899 --> 00:10:13.010
Slowly decreasing the intensity of the surface
wave before it reaches the edge where it would

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:16.950
have released one large return if the tape
was not present.

00:10:16.950 --> 00:10:22.339
The surface of the plane itself is composed
of specialized radar absorbing material.

00:10:22.339 --> 00:10:28.100
In 2010 Lockheed Martin filed this patent
for a carbon nanotube infused composite material

00:10:28.100 --> 00:10:33.720
that can absorb radar waves from 0.1 megahertz
through to the 60 gigahertz.

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:39.080
This is an incredibly wide range of frequencies,
notably covering the frequencies Russian surface

00:10:39.080 --> 00:10:43.280
to air missiles like the advanced S-400 system
uses.

00:10:43.280 --> 00:10:47.510
The effect traveling surface waves have on
stealth design can be seen elsewhere.

00:10:47.510 --> 00:10:52.410
You would imagine a cylinder would great way
to scatter radar in all directions, lowering

00:10:52.410 --> 00:10:57.480
the strength of the return signal, but if
a radar wave comes in tangentially to a cylinder,

00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:02.290
and the wavelength is at least 1/10 the cylinders
circumference, the wave can actually travel

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:06.639
around the outside of the cylinder and travel
straight back to the receiver.

00:11:06.639 --> 00:11:10.160
[3]
This is likely why the F-35 features this

00:11:10.160 --> 00:11:12.880
sharp edge breaking up the circular nose cone.

00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:19.040
Where the f-16 has a nearly perfect circular
nose cone, the F-35 features a ridge.

00:11:19.040 --> 00:11:24.180
The largest hatch on the plane is strangely
not serrated, and this is for good reason.

00:11:24.180 --> 00:11:26.980
This hatch hides the powerful lift fan within.

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:30.920
The air flow in and out of this lift fan needed
careful consideration.

00:11:30.920 --> 00:11:37.160
The lift fan is essentially a tiny helicopter,
capable of generating 85 kN of vertical thrust,

00:11:37.160 --> 00:11:42.290
and in doing so it creates a lower pressure
zone above the plane, violently sucking air

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:45.150
into the aircraft at a 90 degree angle.

00:11:45.150 --> 00:11:49.910
This air has to travel over the hood to reach
the inlet, and thus the hood needs to allow

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:54.730
air to smoothly pass over it without creating
too much turbulent flow, which would lower

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:57.010
the performance of the lift fan.

00:11:57.010 --> 00:12:01.480
This is why the inlet door is not serrated,
as the sharp edges would cause distortions

00:12:01.480 --> 00:12:02.800
in the flow.

00:12:02.800 --> 00:12:07.410
This hood alone went through several design
iterations to optimize the airflow flowing

00:12:07.410 --> 00:12:08.509
by it.

00:12:08.509 --> 00:12:14.120
The demonstration X-35 aircraft had two doors
opening towards the side of the aircraft,

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:18.810
but this was changed for the final aircraft
in favor of a rear hinged door.

00:12:18.810 --> 00:12:23.779
This helps funnel air into the engine and
improves pressure recovery on short take-offs

00:12:23.779 --> 00:12:29.279
where the F-35B does not take off vertically,
instead using the lift fan and directional

00:12:29.279 --> 00:12:33.709
thrust of the rear nozzle to take off on extremely
short runways.

00:12:33.709 --> 00:12:39.480
The way this aircraft transforms to perform
vertical landing and short takeoffs is astounding.

00:12:39.480 --> 00:12:43.570
It’s the closest thing to a transformer
we have ever created.

00:12:43.570 --> 00:12:49.340
When the time comes, a clutch linking the
extended driveshaft of the F-35s engine begins

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:54.890
to transfer 29,000 horsepower to the bevel-gear
of the lift fan..

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:57.399
Spinning the contra-rotating lift fan.

00:12:57.399 --> 00:13:02.370
Simultaneously gears begin to rotate in the
rear exhaust nozzle.

00:13:02.370 --> 00:13:05.950
The mechanics of this nozzle are another wonder
to behold.

00:13:05.950 --> 00:13:07.800
Called the three bearing swivel nozzle.

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:12.420
It is composed of three airtight segments
cut at angles relative to one another.

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:18.430
[4] We can change the shape of the nozzle
by rotating these angled pieces separately.

00:13:18.430 --> 00:13:22.420
These 3 pieces rotate together to smoothly
transition thrust downwards, but it’s slightly

00:13:22.420 --> 00:13:28.589
easier to understand how it works by seeing
what happens when we rotate segments individually.

00:13:28.589 --> 00:13:33.720
Rotating the central piece can take the nozzle
from a zero degree turn to 45 degrees.

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:38.310
This position is used for short take-offs
splitting the engines power between thrust

00:13:38.310 --> 00:13:39.450
and lift.

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:42.680
This mode is just as impressive as vertical
take offs.

00:13:42.680 --> 00:13:46.980
Allowing the plane to operate from shorter
amphibious assault ships like the USS Makin

00:13:46.980 --> 00:13:51.260
Island, a ship just 258 meters long.

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:55.190
Its minimum take-off distance decreases even
further with the aid of a ski-jump.

00:13:55.190 --> 00:14:00.090
A ramp that has been added to smaller amphibious
assault ships.

00:14:00.090 --> 00:14:04.940
Watching the F-35 take off over such short
distances is incredible.

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:10.000
In this clip we can see the rear nozzle quickly
adjusting its angle of thrust, in sync with

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:15.470
the rear elevators, to adjust the pitch of
the plane to ensure a safe take-off.

00:14:15.470 --> 00:14:21.600
For a vertical landing the final nozzle segment
can rotate to provide the full 90 degree turn.

00:14:21.600 --> 00:14:26.440
The first nozzle segment can rotate to move
the nozzle side to side, but here it’s needed

00:14:26.440 --> 00:14:31.130
to ensure that the thrust doesn’t move sideways
as the nozzle transitions.

00:14:31.130 --> 00:14:36.190
As we saw when we rotate the segments individually
they move in an arc that would cause the F-35

00:14:36.190 --> 00:14:39.070
to spin out of control.

00:14:39.070 --> 00:14:45.000
This mechanism also allows the F-35 to smoothly
transition from cruise to vertical flight

00:14:45.000 --> 00:14:47.740
and from vertical flight to cruise when needed.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:52.510
There are additional control mechanisms to
ensure this precarious balancing act does

00:14:52.510 --> 00:14:53.870
not go wrong.

00:14:53.870 --> 00:14:59.940
Bleed air from the main engine bypass is siphoned
to two roll nozzles located on each wing.

00:14:59.940 --> 00:15:04.630
Providing thrust far away from the plane's
center of pressure to control roll.

00:15:04.630 --> 00:15:07.990
There are also guide vanes located underneath
the lift fan.

00:15:07.990 --> 00:15:13.680
This can adjust the outlet area to adjust
the performance of the liftvan, but also control

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:18.959
the thrust of the lift fan from 5 degrees
forward to 42 degrees backward.

00:15:18.959 --> 00:15:25.050
With computer assisted control of these control
mechanisms the F-35 is remarkably stable compared

00:15:25.050 --> 00:15:28.339
to its predecessor the AV-8B Harrier.

00:15:28.339 --> 00:15:33.200
Allowing the single engine of the F-35 to
hover on two columns of air.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:40.529
To do this the F-35s engine, the F-135, developed
from the F-119 engine of the F-22 raptor,

00:15:40.529 --> 00:15:42.750
had to be incredibly powerful.

00:15:42.750 --> 00:15:45.880
The F-22 raptor is a twin engined fighter.

00:15:45.880 --> 00:15:49.950
Giving it plenty of excess power to pull off
incredible maneuvers.

00:15:49.950 --> 00:15:55.680
The F-35 only has one engine, and needs to
squeeze out all the power it can get to perform

00:15:55.680 --> 00:15:57.350
a vertical landing.

00:15:57.350 --> 00:16:03.680
Where the F-22’s engine can generate 156
kilonewtons of thrust, the F-35s can generate

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:05.630
191.

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:11.019
The F-35's engine has a much larger fan and
bypass ducting, giving it twice the bypass

00:16:11.019 --> 00:16:14.089
ratio of its F-22 counterpart.

00:16:14.089 --> 00:16:19.110
Providing the F-35 with a more efficient engine
for cruise, but also a much higher mass flow

00:16:19.110 --> 00:16:21.020
rate for higher thrust.

00:16:21.020 --> 00:16:23.580
However this does come with some drawbacks.

00:16:23.580 --> 00:16:29.399
Air that travels around the engine core completely
avoids the combustion chamber, and thus misses

00:16:29.399 --> 00:16:32.649
out on the acceleration generated here.

00:16:32.649 --> 00:16:34.529
Reducing the exhaust velocity.

00:16:34.529 --> 00:16:37.070
This reduces the top speed of the plane.

00:16:37.070 --> 00:16:42.529
The max speed of the F-35 is 1.6 mach, and
while it’s the first plane capable of vertical

00:16:42.529 --> 00:16:47.670
flight and supersonic flight, the F-22 can
fly at 2.2 mach.

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:51.709
The F-35 was optimized for loiter time, not
speed.

00:16:51.709 --> 00:16:57.519
However, all that extra weight needed for
vertical take off seriously hampers that ideology.

00:16:57.519 --> 00:17:03.430
The liftfan module weighs 1.2 tonnes, weight
that does nothing in normal flight and requires

00:17:03.430 --> 00:17:09.400
more fuel to carry, and to make matters worse
that space is used for an internal fuel tank

00:17:09.400 --> 00:17:11.010
for its variants.

00:17:11.010 --> 00:17:16.290
Making the lift fan as light as possible was
pertinent to making the F-35B viable in the

00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:17.290
battlefield.

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:21.420
The fact it only weighs 1.2 tonnes is astounding.

00:17:21.420 --> 00:17:24.720
It contains two counter-rotating titanium
blisks.

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:29.299
Blisk meaning the blades and disk are all
one single piece, instead of the traditional

00:17:29.299 --> 00:17:34.570
alternative of creating a disk and attaching
blades through dovetail connections.

00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:39.660
This improves efficiency and eliminates a
potential site of failure in the connection.

00:17:39.660 --> 00:17:44.620
This is an astounding feat of manufacturing,
and the first stage fan takes it even further.

00:17:44.620 --> 00:17:47.880
The first stage blades are hollow to save
weight.

00:17:47.880 --> 00:17:53.870
Where the F-35B truly comes into its own however,
is in it’s modern suite of sensors and computers,

00:17:53.870 --> 00:17:55.470
all feeding into this.

00:17:55.470 --> 00:17:59.880
The heads up display incorporated into the
pilots helmet.

00:17:59.880 --> 00:18:04.120
Traditional heads up displays, like those
of the F-16, are incorporated into a panel

00:18:04.120 --> 00:18:05.340
in the cockpit.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:09.840
A panel which the pilot cannot see when scanning
their environment.

00:18:09.840 --> 00:18:14.090
Situational awareness is everything in the
heat of combat, and this helmet does everything

00:18:14.090 --> 00:18:16.510
it can to keep the pilot informed.

00:18:16.510 --> 00:18:20.039
Even giving them x-ray vision and night vision.

00:18:20.039 --> 00:18:24.091
Information from a suite of sensors around
the plane feed into a central computer, where

00:18:24.091 --> 00:18:28.429
it is processed and displayed through a projector
inside the helmet.

00:18:28.429 --> 00:18:33.520
Inside this transparent faceted box underneath
the aircraft is a suite of sensors.

00:18:33.520 --> 00:18:35.650
Those are not your typical windows.

00:18:35.650 --> 00:18:40.350
These windows are made from a notoriously
expensive gemstone, sapphire.

00:18:40.350 --> 00:18:45.010
One of the few materials that is both hard
and durable, but also transparent to a broad

00:18:45.010 --> 00:18:47.370
range of electromagnetic wavelengths.

00:18:47.370 --> 00:18:49.510
From the ultraviolet to infrared.

00:18:49.510 --> 00:18:55.230
However, the radar antenna hidden inside the
nose of the F-35 is the most important part

00:18:55.230 --> 00:18:57.309
of this electronics system.

00:18:57.309 --> 00:19:03.250
This is a scanned array radar that works very
differently to traditional mechanical radar.

00:19:03.250 --> 00:19:06.320
Phased array antennas have hundreds of tiny
antennas.

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:11.169
We can see metal plates set in rows in the
F-35 phase array antenna.

00:19:11.169 --> 00:19:15.870
The metal plates have slots cut into them,
and each and every one of these slots is an

00:19:15.870 --> 00:19:19.289
antenna. 1600 in total.

00:19:19.289 --> 00:19:25.510
This allows the phase array antenna to steer
its radar using constructive and destructive

00:19:25.510 --> 00:19:26.510
interference.

00:19:26.510 --> 00:19:30.740
If two antennas release two radar waves at
the exact same time, with their peaks and

00:19:30.740 --> 00:19:36.299
troughs line up, it will result in constructive
interference, increasing the amplitude of

00:19:36.299 --> 00:19:37.590
the radio wave.

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:43.760
However if the radio waves are set 180 degrees
out of phase, matching the peak to troughs

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:49.570
it will result in complete destructive interference,
canceling out the wave completely.

00:19:49.570 --> 00:19:51.860
This is how noise canceling earphones work.

00:19:51.860 --> 00:19:57.070
They listen to the background noise and then
release a canceling sound wave to create silence.

00:19:57.070 --> 00:20:02.770
The phased array antenna uses this phenomenon
to steer the radio waves, preventing the radar

00:20:02.770 --> 00:20:07.180
from becoming a giant beacon leading enemies
straight to it.

00:20:07.180 --> 00:20:09.390
Traditional phase array antennas are passive.

00:20:09.390 --> 00:20:14.809
Meaning every antenna in the array is driven
by a single transmitter and receiver.

00:20:14.809 --> 00:20:20.270
This would mean it can only point in one direction,
and if it encountered two enemy planes flying

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:25.250
side by side, and they split up, the passive
phase array antenna would no longer be able

00:20:25.250 --> 00:20:27.020
to track both of them.

00:20:27.020 --> 00:20:33.159
However the F-35’s phase antenna is an active
phased array, meaning each and everyone of

00:20:33.159 --> 00:20:37.600
these antennas is an individually driven transmitter
and receiver.

00:20:37.600 --> 00:20:43.169
Meaning the F-35 can track multiple targets
at once with zero moving parts.

00:20:43.169 --> 00:20:47.670
The nose cones hiding these antennas need
to be transparent to radar waves, and are

00:20:47.670 --> 00:20:50.770
usually made from glass fiber composites as
a result.

00:20:50.770 --> 00:20:55.550
This transparency causes issues for the plane's
radar return signature, as an antenna like

00:20:55.550 --> 00:20:57.850
this will reflect signals.

00:20:57.850 --> 00:21:02.679
This was a much bigger issue for mechanical
radar dishes that needed to point at the enemy

00:21:02.679 --> 00:21:04.039
to keep track of them.

00:21:04.039 --> 00:21:08.650
The phased array can point towards multiple
targets while staying in a single position,

00:21:08.650 --> 00:21:11.660
and this is why it is pointed skywards.

00:21:11.660 --> 00:21:14.560
To bounce incoming radar to space.

00:21:14.560 --> 00:21:18.610
The phased array antenna also acts as the
planes communication antenna, and this is

00:21:18.610 --> 00:21:21.650
critical to the F-35s battle doctrine.

00:21:21.650 --> 00:21:26.799
The F-35 excels in the battlefield because
of its networking abilities.

00:21:26.799 --> 00:21:29.390
Relaying information between it’s squadron

00:21:29.390 --> 00:21:33.900
This is a huge amount of data to transfer
between aircraft and allies on the surface,

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:36.529
and requires a high data transfer speed.

00:21:36.529 --> 00:21:39.620
However, communication comes with one glaring
problem.

00:21:39.620 --> 00:21:42.720
It announces your presence to anyone listening.

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:47.891
It is vital that stealth planes can communicate
with each other securely, and the active phase

00:21:47.891 --> 00:21:50.460
array antenna facilitates this.

00:21:50.460 --> 00:21:56.100
The F-35 uses the latest data link system
called MADL, improving on the experiences

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:58.380
learned with the F-22.

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:05.390
Allowing the F-35 to quickly share data securely
from individual F-35s and ground based systems.

00:22:05.390 --> 00:22:11.020
This information is then sorted and presented
to the pilot in their heads up display right

00:22:11.020 --> 00:22:12.490
in front of their eyes.

00:22:12.490 --> 00:22:16.039
Giving them unparalleled situational awareness.

00:22:16.039 --> 00:22:20.600
No need to communicate with their wingmates
if there is an adversary underneath them,

00:22:20.600 --> 00:22:25.330
the planes communicate and feed that data
right into the helmet allowing the pilot to

00:22:25.330 --> 00:22:30.220
look underneath the plane and see the location
of the adversary themselves.

00:22:30.220 --> 00:22:32.919
This is the true strength of the F-35B.

00:22:32.919 --> 00:22:37.799
It is a networked hivemind stealth fighter
capable of taking off from a ship a fraction

00:22:37.799 --> 00:22:42.679
of the size of an aircraft carrier, and returning
while hovering in the sky like a helicopter.

00:22:42.679 --> 00:22:48.330
It’s one of the most remarkable pieces of
military technology ever created.

00:22:48.330 --> 00:22:54.669
The F-35 has borrowed many lessons learned
from planes like the F-117 Nighthawk and B-2,

00:22:54.669 --> 00:22:59.220
